#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129938} :activity/id #uuid "5f586b25-2c9f-53c9-8c70-a938202d5eda" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048980} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129938 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130339 :tutorial/content "Consider the following matrices:\n\n\n$$\nA = \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 6 & 7 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 6 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 4 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5 & 8\\end{bmatrix}, \\qquad B = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 4 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n\n\nThe first non-zero entry on each row (from left to right) is called the **leading entry** of that row.\n\n- In the matrix $A,$ the leading entries are $a_{11}=2,$ $a_{22}=3,$ $a_{32}=1,$ and $a_{43}=5.$\n\n- In the matrix $B,$ the leading entries are $b_{11}=1,$ $b_{22}=3,$ $b_{33}=-2,$ and $b_{44}=2.$ The $5$th row has no leading entry.\n\nThe leading entries are highlighted below.\n\n\n\n$$\nA = \\begin{bmatrix}\\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{2} & 6 & 7 & 8 \\\\ {\\color{blue}0} & \\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{3} & 6 & 9 \\\\ {\\color{blue}0} & \\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{{\\color{red}1}} & 4 & 7 \\\\ {\\color{blue}0} & {\\color{blue}0} & \\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{5} & 8\\end{bmatrix}, \\qquad B = \\begin{bmatrix}\\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{1} & 5 & 2 & 0 \\\\ {\\color{blue}0} & \\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{3} & 4 & 3 \\\\ {\\color{blue}0} & {\\color{blue}0} & \\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{-2} & 1 \\\\ {\\color{blue}0} & {\\color{blue}0} & {\\color{blue}0} & \\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{2} \\\\ {\\color{blue}0} & {\\color{blue}0} & {\\color{blue}0} & {\\color{blue}0}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n" :tutorial/id #uuid "24451a80-db9f-5a0f-a591-41500a69774c" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3148 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "5cf43dee-1fef-5d4c-9775-65a5c7ed9dc1" :step/math-academy-id 17175 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130042}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129939 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129113 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128925 :question/id #uuid "674e7733-7a8e-5804-a867-82c74c583dcc" :question/math-academy-id "e-4687" :question/problem "Which of the following augmented matrices are in row echelon form?\n\n$$\n\\left[\n\t\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 1\\end{array}\n\t\\right]\n$$\n\n$$\n\\left[\n\t\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 2 & -1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 7\\end{array}\n\t\\right]\n$$\n\n$$\n\\left[\n\t\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 0 & -1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{array}\n\t\\right]\n$$\n\n$$\n\\left[\n\t\\begin{array}{ccc|c}0 & 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1 & 1\\end{array}\n\t\\right].\n$$\n\n" :question/worked-solution "Remember that an augmented matrix is in row echelon form if its coefficient matrix satisfies the following conditions:\n\n- All the zero rows (rows with only zeros), if any, are placed at the *bottom* of the matrix.\n\n- Each leading entry of a row is in a column *to the right* of the leading entry of the row above it.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each matrix in turn.\n\n- The first matrix is not in row echelon form since $a_{32} \\neq 0$ (condition II is violated).\n\n$$\n\\left[\n\t\\begin{array}{ccc|c}\\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{1} & 2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ {\\color{blue}0} & \\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{1} & 0 & 1 \\\\ {\\color{blue}0} & {\\color{red}1} & 1 & 1\\end{array}\n\t\\right]\n$$\n\n- The second matrix is in row echelon form (both conditions are satisfied).\n\n$$\n\\left[\n\t\\begin{array}{ccc|c}\\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{1} & 2 & -1 & 5 \\\\ {\\color{blue}0} & \\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{2} & 0 & -2 \\\\ {\\color{blue}0} & {\\color{blue}0} & \\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{1} & 7\\end{array}\n\t\\right]\n$$\n\n- The third matrix is in row echelon form (both conditions are satisfied).\n\n$$\n\\left[\n\t\\begin{array}{ccc|c}\\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{1} & 0 & -1 & 4 \\\\ {\\color{blue}0} & \\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{1} & 0 & 1 \\\\ {\\color{blue}0} & {\\color{blue}0} & {\\color{blue}0} & 0\\end{array}\n\t\\right]\n$$\n\n- The fourth matrix is not in row echelon form since, for example, the leading entry $a_{22}$ lies to the left of the leading entry $a_{13}$ (condition II is violated).\n\n$$\n\\left[\n\t\\begin{array}{ccc|c}0 & 0 & \\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{1} & 1 \\\\ 0 & \\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{{\\color{red}1}} & {\\color{black}0} & 1 \\\\ \\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{{\\color{red}1}} & {\\color{red}1} & {\\color{red}1} & 1\\end{array}\n\t\\right]\n$$\n\n"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f5d1d859-0b86-5507-a7bf-3d805a85adf3" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050765}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186657705} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129771} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129774} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262994} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262996} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262997} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326923} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341835} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341837} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341838} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400436} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405146} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413825} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562460} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562495} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562509} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186618941} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186618944} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186618945} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186618959} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186618979}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Augmented Matrices in Row Echelon Form"} :step/id #uuid "9da7e60b-1218-59ef-a17a-68019b1df9c5" :step/math-academy-id 17176 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129941}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129940 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129114 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128926 :question/id #uuid "b6e4983b-c695-5944-9637-6d7547dae849" :question/math-academy-id "e-4688" :question/problem "Which of the following matrices are in row echelon form?\n\n$$\nA= \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n$$\nB= \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 & 7 & 10 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n$$\nC= \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 4 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 & 3 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 4 & 8\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n" :question/worked-solution "Remember that a matrix is in row echelon form if it satisfies the following conditions:\n\n- All the zero rows (rows with only zeros), if any, are placed at the *bottom* of the matrix.\n\n- Each leading entry of a row is in a column *to the right* of the leading entry of the row above it.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each matrix in turn.\n\n- The matrix $A$ is not in row echelon form since the second row contains only zeros and it is not at the bottom of the matrix (condition I is violated).\n\n$$\nA= \\begin{bmatrix}\\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{2} & 1 \\\\ {\\color{red}0} & {\\color{red}0} \\\\ 0 & \\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{3}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n- The matrix $B$ is in row echelon form (both conditions are satisfied).\n\n$$\nB= \\begin{bmatrix}{\\color{blue}0} & \\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{2} & 7 & 10 \\\\ {\\color{blue}0} & {\\color{blue}0} & {\\color{blue}0} & {\\color{blue}0}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n- The matrix $C$ is in row echelon form (both conditions are satisfied).\n\n$$\nC= \\begin{bmatrix}\\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{2} & 0 & 0 & 0 & 4 & 6 \\\\ {\\color{blue}0} & {\\color{blue}0} & \\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{3} & 3 & 6 & 0 \\\\ {\\color{blue}0} & {\\color{blue}0} & {\\color{blue}0} & {\\color{blue}0} & \\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{4} & 8\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2d390841-351e-54f5-ad19-90b6ebae5799" :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186657706} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129773} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129778} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262985} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262990} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262993} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339126} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339127} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400408} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400415} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400422} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402796} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403578} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403585} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413824} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186618992} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186618996} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186619004} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186619015} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186619025}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Matrices in Row Echelon Form"} :step/id #uuid "d00efb7b-6c6d-5f75-a839-fd2c4b29adfc" :step/math-academy-id 17177 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129939}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129941 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129115 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128927 :question/id #uuid "97237f8e-b317-5c86-86d1-8fd3cc83e66b" :question/math-academy-id "e-4689" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A$ given below.\nWhich entries of $A$ could be replaced by $0$ in order to get a matrix in row echelon form?\n\n$$\nA = \\begin{bmatrix}3 & 11 & 10 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 7 & 8 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 6 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n" :question/worked-solution "Notice that by replacing $a_{32}$, $a_{42}$, and $a_{43}$ with $0$, we get a matrix in row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A  &= \\begin{bmatrix}\\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{3} & 11 & 10 & 9 \\\\ {\\color{blue}0} & \\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{4} & 7 & 8 \\\\ {\\color{blue}0} & \\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{{\\color{red}-1}} & \\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{6} & 5 \\\\ {\\color{blue}0} & \\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{{\\color{red}1}} & \\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{{\\color{red}2}} & 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ &\\longrightarrow \\begin{bmatrix}\\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{3} & 11 & 10 & 9 \\\\ {\\color{blue}0} & \\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{4} & 7 & 8 \\\\ {\\color{blue}0} & {\\color{blue}0} & \\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{6} & 5 \\\\ {\\color{blue}0} & {\\color{blue}0} & {\\color{blue}0} & \\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{4}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fe0050b2-b5bc-54a6-aeaf-94cd5d7e5dfb" :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186671463} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129772} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129779} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265724} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265725} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329643} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341915} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341916} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341917} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402803} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413822} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562399} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562463} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562485} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186619068} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186619071} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186619077} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186619080} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186619098} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186639177} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186639237} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186671428} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186671432} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186671433} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186671435} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186671438}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Operations That Would Transform a Matrix To Row Echelon Form"} :step/id #uuid "ed25462b-dcf5-53b8-af91-93afa32dea4c" :step/math-academy-id 17178} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130041 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130380 :tutorial/content "Let's consider our matrices $A$ and $B$ once more, with the leading entries highlighted.\n\n\n\n$$\nA = \\begin{bmatrix}\\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{2} & 6 & 7 & 8 \\\\ {\\color{blue}0} & \\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{3} & 6 & 9 \\\\ {\\color{blue}0} & \\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{{\\color{red}1}} & 4 & 7 \\\\ {\\color{blue}0} & {\\color{blue}0} & \\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{5} & 8\\end{bmatrix}, \\qquad B = \\begin{bmatrix}\\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{1} & 5 & 2 & 0 \\\\ {\\color{blue}0} & \\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{3} & 4 & 3 \\\\ {\\color{blue}0} & {\\color{blue}0} & \\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{-2} & 1 \\\\ {\\color{blue}0} & {\\color{blue}0} & {\\color{blue}0} & \\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{2} \\\\ {\\color{blue}0} & {\\color{blue}0} & {\\color{blue}0} & {\\color{blue}0}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n\n\nWe say that a matrix is in **row echelon form** if it satisfies the following conditions:\n\nI. All the zero rows (rows with only zeros), if any, are placed at the *bottom* of the matrix.\n\nII. Each leading entry of a row is in a column *to the right* of the leading entry of the row above it.\n\nHere, $B$ is in row echelon form since both conditions are satisfied. However, $A$ is not in row echelon form since condition II is violated: the leading entry of the third row $(a_{32}={\\color{red}1})$ is placed just below the leading entry of the second row $(a_{22}=3).$\n\nGenerally speaking, row echelon form means that we have *stairs* where each step (represented by a leading entry $\\bbox[lightgray, 3pt]{\\large\\ast}$) has a height of $1.$\n\n![](images/67/67f83c454e59d16ae80cf67a19b19f968182251a2e5d1b405e2c422e0313d5b7.png)\n\nFinally, an *augmented* matrix is in row echelon form if the coefficient matrix (i.e., the part that is to the left of the vertical bar) is in row echelon form." :tutorial/id #uuid "137b0909-9a5e-5edf-8019-24e803307903" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3896 :tutorial/title "Row Echelon Form"} :step/id #uuid "1430eddc-7fc0-5265-81de-85b9a44bbf70" :step/math-academy-id 23024 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129940}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130042 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129176 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128995 :question/id #uuid "7fd5c01f-c20e-5c47-b8d9-8cfbe634c14d" :question/math-academy-id "e-6166" :question/problem "What is the sum of the leading entries of the $1$st, $3$rd and $4$th rows in the matrix $A,$ given below?\n\n$$\nA=\n\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 7 & 8 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 8 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n" :question/worked-solution "The first non-zero entry of each row (from left to right) is the leading entry. \n\nTherefore, the leading entries of the $1$st, $3$rd and $4$th rows of $A$ are $-1$, $-4$ and $8$, respectively.\n\n$$\nA\n=\\begin{bmatrix}\\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{-1} & 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 7 & 8 \\\\ 0 & \\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{-4} & 2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{8} & 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSo, the answer is $-1+(-4)+8=3.$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "31c3678b-4f00-54d6-bb84-c7718fd622cb" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050163}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186671499} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129776} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129777} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262981} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262982} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262984} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329642} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341839} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341911} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341912} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341913} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341914} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413823} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562388} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562461} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186619105} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186619112} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186619127} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186619132} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186619133} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186639176} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186671468} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186671485} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186671486} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186671487} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186671495}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Leading Entries of a Matrix"} :step/id #uuid "f062c6b3-17e0-57cb-a070-e9edcc0af6c8" :step/math-academy-id 23025 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130041}}] :activity/title "Row Echelon Form" :activity/type {:db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186129395}]}
